WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:09.170
July 1965: the first close-up pictures of Mars arrive on Earth as long lists of numbers.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:15.060
The engineers couldn't wait for the computers. They coloured the numbers in by hand.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:24.340
In 1971, Mariner 9 became the first spacecraft to orbit another planet.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:32.850
In 1999, Mars Climate Orbiter was lost: one team used pound-force, the other used newtons.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:37.850
It flew far too close to Mars and was never heard from again.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:44.310
To stay at Mars, a spacecraft must brake as it arrives.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:54.750
Then, for five months, Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter dipped into the thin upper air again and again, shrinking its orbit with the air instead of fuel.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:06.920
Since 2006 it has studied Mars from about 300 kilometres up, with a camera that can spot things the size of a kitchen table.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.390
Now compare your design. And always check your units.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:17.870
<v Goicochea>Paulet dreamed of rockets. We are reading the weather on another planet.

